      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Optical flow for motion segmentation\n\nFind what moves DIFFERENTLY from the dominant scene motion. Here: two consecutive frames of a STATIC traffic camera, so the road is the dominant (near-zero) motion and a single crossing car is the only thing that moves.\n\nPipeline:\n1. Dense optical flow (Farneback) between frame A and frame B.\n2. 'CV Array Statistic' (MEDIAN) reduces the HxWx2 flow to the dominant motion vector - the background / camera motion (~0 for a fixed camera). The median is robust: a moving object can contaminate a large minority of pixels without dragging it.\n3. residual = flow - dominant (cv2.subtract broadcasts the 2-vector); its magnitude (cartToPolar) is large only where motion departs from the background - the car.\n4. threshold + open -> a noisy mask. 'Keep Largest Component' drops stray motion (other movers, speckle); close + 'Fill Holes' turn it into a SOLID single-car seed (single-pair flow is patchy over the low-texture roof/hood).\n\n( Additional Refinement via GrabCut )\n5. Erode the solid seed to a confident core (probable foreground) and dilate-then-invert it (gap = 1) into a TIGHT sure-background ring, so GrabCut refines the car's colour boundary without bleeding into the shadow.\n6. Clean the GrabCut output the same way (Fill Holes + Keep Largest Component + a median blur to smooth the edge), then cv2.bitwise_and cuts the car out.\n\nDebug views: flow color wheel, residual heatmap, the dominant-motion vector, the solid seed, the GrabCut label map, and the refined mask. Two identical frames (no motion) -> no seed -> success=false and an empty mask, no error."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
